e chose to study this with  george Sagan's and very Hoffer in the  early 70s was in the cerebellum because  Sir John Eccles and his colleagues had  made that the best understood place in  the brain for synaptic transmitter  circuitry and so when we applied Noor  for nephron and later on when we  stimulated its origins and the locus  coeruleus we found that we could  simulate all the effects of  norepinephrine by five Beryl  micropipettes doing agonist we could  block the effects of the locus coeruleus  and the effects of applied  norepinephrine with beta adrenergic  blockers and we could extend the effects  of norepinephrine through ad noelite  cyclase by stimuli by releasing closely  cyclic AMP E or chemicals which would  extend the lifetime of cyclic AMP E and  I have because of the limits on time  this afternoon eliminated all of the  g-protein coupled receptor discussion  but another major major chapter in our  history of chemical  mission is represented by that now  having identified that norepinephrine  could simulate the effects of the locus  coeruleus Gary Aston Jones and Steve  Foote studied the locus julius's  discharge rate in awake behaving rats  and in awake behaving monkeys and they  found that the locus coeruleus would  respond rapidly to any novel stimulus in  its environment and that this novel  stimulus caused the locus coeruleus to  activate its synaptic transmitter  systems and circuits all over the  central nervous system when we look at  the synapse we can see that when the  locus coeruleus is firing at low rates  it activates alpha adrenergic receptors  which have a high nerve receptor  affinity and when they stimulate  physically they simulate the beta  adrenergic receptors which is what we  observed in the cerebellum so the entire  system from the locus coeruleus to its  synapses in terminals throughout the  central nervous system gave us an  opinion that norepinephrine could be the  neurotransmitter for causing focused  attention and Gary A